  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Minimal Impacts Today: Periodic light precipitation and breezy\n   westerly winds.\n\n-  Wednesday and Thursday Fog/Stratus Potential: Strong high \n   pressure increasing likelihood of valley inversions.\n\n-  Pattern Shift: A Pacific system will tap into atmospheric \n   river moisture beginning Saturday, snow levels stay relatively \n   high.\n\nToday and Tonight: A Pacific system is bringing a wave of light \nprecipitation to the Northern Rockies early this morning, \nproducing up to a tenth of an inch per hour in the favored\nmountains. With snow levels remaining above 6,000 feet, winter \nimpacts will be minimal. Precipitation will transition to \nscattered showers after sunrise. Meanwhile, westerly valley winds \nwill increase late this morning into the afternoon, gusting 15 to \n25 mph before diminishing this evening. Tonight, a glancing blow \nfrom an atmospheric river will push another round of light \nprecipitation into northwest Montana.\n \nWednesday through Sunday: Strong upper-level ridging builds \nacross the Intermountain West Wednesday into Thursday. Mid-level \nwarm air advection and subsidence will yield highly stable \nconditions, setting the stage for valley inversions. The primary \nforecast challenge is the strength of these inversions and their \nsubsequent impact on valley high temperatures, which hinges on the\nextent of overnight fog and stratus Tuesday night into Wednesday \nmorning and again Wednesday night.\n\nAbundant mid- to high-level cloud cover may limit radiational \ncooling, potentially restricting widespread fog development. \nHowever, areas north of I-90, particularly near Flathead Lake, \nwhere warm surface water will enhance boundary layer moisture \nremain the most likely target for fog and stratus. If persistent \nstratus materializes, high temperatures in these localized areas \nwill trend several degrees cooler than currently forecast. \nConversely, higher elevations will break out of the inversions and\nexperience anomalously warm temperatures for this time of year.\n\nBy Friday and into the weekend, ensembles are in excellent \nagreement that the ridge axis will migrate eastward. This \ntransition places the Northern Rockies under a southwesterly flow \nregime, introducing cold air aloft that will help mix out any \nlingering valley inversions on Saturday. Under a broader split- \nflow pattern, an atmospheric river impacting the West Coast will \nadvect a plume of moisture into the Inland Northwest on Saturday. \nSnow levels will remain notably high through the weekend, \ngenerally hovering between 5,500 and 6,500 feet. Precipitation \namounts will be light in the mountains and very light in the \nvalleys, as the Cascades intercept much of the moisture and the \nprimary trough energy digs well south of our region. \n\nNext Week: Ensemble guidance favors a continuation of this split-\nflow pattern through much of next week. Successive upper-level \ntroughs and atmospheric rivers will crash into the West Coast, but\nthe bulk of the dynamic energy will dive south into California \nand the Desert Southwest. Consequently, the Northern Rockies can \nexpect generally seasonable temperatures and relatively light \nprecipitation.",
